From 8c32084b3312331958893fb97034f5f9ae7f6756 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tylnexttime <tylnexttime@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:16:29 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Marian 0008 prefill source: the large-batch selective copy
 takes its misses from the P3 source

With --moe-miss-source ring or tier, llama_context::sched_copy_experts (ubatches above 32 tokens) no longer uploads the
experts it does not copy from the VRAM cache from the memory-mapped host tensor. Each run of experts, and upstream's
512-byte MMQ padding after it, comes from the P3 source instead: an expert the pinned tier holds is uploaded from its
tier slot; the others are read unbuffered (FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING / O_DIRECT) through the pinned staging ring, runs of
consecutive experts in chunks of up to one ring segment, up to 16 reads in flight. The ring is cut into 32 segments; a
segment is reused only after the upload from it has run (a backend event per segment, or a synchronize when the device
has no events), and the large-batch path synchronizes once before using ring memory the slot path may still upload from.
A failed read is uploaded from the host tensor and counted. The bytes and destinations are exactly upstream's (same dst
offsets and lengths, same file bytes the memory map holds); everything is queued on the backend's stream before the
split's compute. Off when the miss source is mmap (the default path is unchanged).

Debug switch: MARIAN_MOE_SOURCE_VERIFY=1 compares the first MARIAN_MOE_SOURCE_VERIFY_N uploads (default 100000; slot
path and large-batch path, tier hits included) with the host tensor and aborts on any difference, reporting its count
every 50000 uploads; =all now compares every upload, tier hits included.

Traces: the P0 L line is unchanged (bytes = bytes uploaded, now from the source); the fill trace
(MARIAN_MOE_TRACE_FILLS) gains one L line per expert tensor uploaded by a selective copy from the source, slot -1, src
t (tier) or r (ring), joined to the copy's L line by (t_us, layer); readers of F lines are unaffected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
